{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1539066880,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUzNjgwNjAxNTE=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2908","origin":"github"},"title":"Creating subdatasets linearly slows with number of subdatasets","message":"Variants of this issue have been reported previously #1874 #2015 (private reports). Here is a demo that demos a particular aspect of this issue. Skript to create 500 straight subdatasets of a single superdataset:\n\n```sh\nset -e -u\n\ndatalad create many\ncd many\nfor i in $(seq 500); do\n\techo $i\n\t/usr/bin/time -o ../create_timing.txt --append -f \"%D\\t%K\\t%M\\t%e\\t%S\\t%U\" datalad create -d . $i\ndone\n```\n\nPlotting the runtime log of each invocation of `create` we can clearly see a trend were the duration more than doubles from the first to the last run:\n\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/136479/46650727-f07fa000-cb9d-11e8-9eed-7efe8cb0576a.png)\n\nA likely source is the particular behavior of path annotation (which is already being addressed in https://github.com/datalad/datalad-revolution), but other explanations are possible.","files":null}]}